  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "If the line $y=-x+5$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+5 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+5 & =\\frac{2}{x-5}+2 \\\\ -x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-5} \\\\ (-x+3)(x-5) & =2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+17 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-8)\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4(1)(17)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{64-68}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-x+5$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/73570088dd6c424f4848b25ce3305edaf1ec50c07039b463f4af9a5dd35bfb43.png)",
